-*- mode: TEXT -*-

MAJOR SYSTEMS AND CLOCKING

	The major systems are shown in the diagram.  The control PLA
recieves its input, clocked on phi1.  This input selects a line of the
OR plane.  All outputs of the control PLA are clocked on phi2. One
signal selects a predicate, The output of the predicate box is the T/F
signal.  One set of control PLA outputs controls the data to be fed to
the global bus control and the local register operations.  Two sets of
signals are developed to control the uarg stack, one will be selected
by the T/F signal.  Two dispatch decoders are attached to the type
field of the register file.  Also, the control PLA develops two
alternate explicit next states.  Two control signals for the NS
machine are developed and selected by the T/F signal.  Among the NS
control signals is the selected source of next state.

	The data paths: the register file, the NS machine, and the
UARG stacks are all clocked as follows:
	They may be loaded during phi1;
	They may be read any time, the value read is the
	value loaded (or restored) during the previous phi1.

THE NEXT-STATE MACHINE

	The next state machine is a register file whose global bus
(which will be called the "NS-BUS") is connected directly to
the STATE-INPUT field of the control PLA.  Thus if anyone gets to
place his value on the NS-BUS, that value will be the next state of
the machine.  That value is also sent to the output lines of the STATE
pads. 

	Each element of the next state machine has the control signals
SOURCE and DISABLE.  They mean:
	SOURCE	put value onto the NS-BUS, unless;
	DISABLE	is on.

	See the figure for the arrangement and clocking of the members
of the next-state machine.  During one cycle of the machine, the
control PLA does the following:
	Selects a pedicate;
	Produces values for TRUE-EXPLICIT and FALSE-EXPLICIT;
	Produces two sets of controls for the NS machine, one for if
the predicate is true, one for if it is false.

	The PREDICATE machine produces predicates as boolean
combinations of "primitive" predicates from the register file and
pads.  The selected predicate is used to produce the T/F signal.  This
signal is used to select between the two NS control signals.  The
selected NS control signal selects which element of the NS machine
gets to source the NS bus and, possibly, controls the operation of the
stack.

	If the PC-FREEZE signal is on, all sources of NS except PC are
disabled, and, if LOAD&FREEZE is low, PC is selected to source NS.  If
LOAD&FREEZE is on, NS always comes from the pads. LOAD&FREEZE causes
the tri-state STATE pads to become input pads.

	NS Machine Elements:

	PC is a register that always gets the value from the NS bus,
gated by phi1.  This value is available to be sourced back onto the
bus for the next cycle.

	INC always increments the current value in PC.

	STACK's only push-source is the value in INC.

	TRUE-EXPLICIT and FALSE-EXPLICIT come directly (clocked on
phi2) from the control PLA.

	DISPATCH-USER-TYPE and DISPATCH-EXTRA-TYPE-CONT come from
decoders connected to the TYPE field of the REGISTER file.

	PADS gets its value from the input lines of the STATE pads.


